<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Van Fortress</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Van Kalesi, Rock of Van</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Van Fortress, or Van Kalesi, is a massive citadel on a long limestone ridge on the eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. It was the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tushpa">Tushpa</a>, the capital of Urartu from the ninth century BCE, and preserves rock-cut tombs and inscriptions, including the annals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argishti-i">Argishti I</a>. It was later held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and others, and the old city of Van lay at its foot until its destruction in 1915.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/van-fortress.jpg" alt="Van Fortress" width="320" height="134" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bjørn Christian Tørrissen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Van_Fortress_From_Northwest.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
